Copy an existing canvas element (with its whole subtree) without re-sending its JSX. USE THIS FOR VARIATIONS: when the user asks for another version of a screen, section, card or row that already exists, duplicate it and edit the copy — never redraw it from scratch, and never canvas_read a large tree just to paste it back through canvas_add (big trees come back truncated). The copy gets fresh ids at every node.

Placement: by default the copy lands next to its source — a top-level frame takes the nearest free slot beside the original (right of it, else below it), a child becomes the next sibling. Pass before_id / after_id / parent_id (+ optional index) to put it somewhere else.

Claiming: pass claim_new=true whenever you intend to edit the copy — it returns a claim_id for the copy so you can edit it straight away. Without it you must canvas_read the whole copy before canvas_claim will accept it, which is the expensive read this tool exists to avoid. Then canvas_grep / canvas_query with root_id set to the new id to find what to change, and canvas_edit only that. claim_id (not claim_new) is required when the copy lands inside a parent — claim that parent first.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__lunagraph__canvas_duplicate(args: {
  // Optional: place the copy directly after this element
  after_id?: string;
  // Optional: place the copy directly before this element
  before_id?: string;
  // claim_id covering the destination parent. Required when the copy lands inside a parent.
  claim_id?: string;
  // Claim the copy in this call and return its claim_id, so you can edit it immediately. Do not combine with claim_id.
  claim_new?: boolean;
  // Element to copy (its whole subtree comes with it)
  element_id: string;
  // Optional child index when using parent_id (default: append)
  index?: number;
  // Optional: place the copy inside this element
  parent_id?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

